README.OSK -- how this tree differs from 4.3BSD-Net/2's usr/src/games/trekORIG/ is the tree exactly as Net/2 carried it: Star Trek, the C version byEric P. Allman (UC Berkeley, 1976), in the University of California's 1990release.  obj (a symlink into the build machine) and tags are not carried.DOC/ holds the troff sources of its manual and notes.The game itself is unchanged.  Four files carry -DOSK guards:  main.c       no <sgtty.h>, and no gtty() speed test.  On a terminal slower               than 1200 baud that test chose the terse "fast" mode; -f               still chooses it.  dumpgame.c   creat() gets OS-9's owner read and write (3), not Unix's 0644,               which as an OS-9 attribute byte includes $80, the directory               bit; open() for the restore uses read mode 1, where Unix's 0               grants no access here.  The save file is trek.dump in the               current directory, as it always was.  trek.h       its globals -- Quad, Sect, Event, Trace and the Ship, Game,               Move, Param, Now and Etc structures -- are prefixed EXTERN,               which is extern on OS-9.  The header defined them in every               file that includes it; a Unix linker merges those, and l68               reports each as `has already appeared'.  Device[] is               declared extern outright: externs.c defines it with its               initializer, and c68 takes a definition in the header plus               that one as a multiple definition in one file.  externs.c    defines EXTERN as nothing before including trek.h, so it               is the one file that holds the definitions.The recipe links /dd/LIB/rand.r, because ranf() shifts rand() right by fivebits and expects 31 of them, and blarslib.l for atan2().